# `unionfind` from Lean 4's own code-generator benchmarks, as shipped by
# lean-to-lambdabox (benchmarks/FromLeanCommon/unionfind.lean, revision 58701f8):
# builds n union-find nodes in an array, merges them in several passes, then counts
# the equivalence classes. NOT our program: unionfind.lean is a verbatim copy,
# prog.lean only closes it over its input, and prog.ast is its unmodified λ□
# output.
#
# Why it is worth having: it is the only program written in a STATE MONAD
# (`M = ExceptT Error (StateM ...)`, with fuel), so the λ□ it produces is a chain
# of closures threading a state — the higher-order shape that made lean-deriv 10x
# slower than OCaml before the Fn2/Fn3 work, but over arrays instead of an
# expression tree. It also uses `Array.set!`/`Array.get!Internal` destructively in
# Lean, which both backends must realize persistently.
#
# Input 5000 instead of upstream's 100000; the merge passes (50000/10000/5000/1000
# iterations, hard-coded in the benchmark) do not scale with the input, so the
# reduction only shrinks the array, not the work per pass.
#
# 4000 is a genuine result, not an error code: the benchmark returns
# 413/404/666/400 for out-of-fuel/invalid-node/not-equal/input<2, so a wrong
# backend is likely to be caught by one of those rather than by a wrong count.
expected=4000
backends=java ocaml
runtime=lean
xfail=
note=equivalence classes; upstream unionfind at n=5000 (413/404/666/400 would be error codes)
